🔷 Step-by-step: Connect Gateway to Eureka

🔹 1. Dependencies (Gateway pom.xml)
```xml
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-gateway</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-netflix-eureka-client</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- usually needed for lb:// (often transitive, but safe to add) -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-loadbalancer</artifactId>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>

<dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-cloud-dependencies</artifactId>
            <version>2023.0.1</version> <!-- match your Boot version -->
            <type>pom</type>
            <scope>import</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
</dependencyManagement>
```


🔹 2. application.yml
```yaml
server:
  port: 8080

spring:
  application:
    name: api-gateway
  cloud:
    gateway:
      # Option A: explicit routes (recommended for interviews/production control)
      routes:
        - id: user-service
          uri: lb://user-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/users/**
          filters:
            - StripPrefix=1

        - id: order-service
          uri: lb://order-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/orders/**
          filters:
            - StripPrefix=1

      # Option B: auto routes by service id (optional)
      discovery:
        locator:
          enabled: false          # set true only if you want auto /{service-id}/** routes
          lower-case-service-id: true

eureka:
  client:
    service-url:
      defaultZone: http://localhost:8761/eureka/
```

What `lb://user-service` means:
Use LoadBalancer + Eureka to resolve a real instance of `user-service`.


🧭 What is Discovery Locator? (simple)

Locator = Gateway automatically creates routes from Eureka service names.
You do NOT write those routes yourself.

There are 2 ways to route:

────────────────────────────────────
Way A — Explicit routes (YOU write them)  ✅ common in real projects
```yaml
routes:
  - id: user-service
    uri: lb://user-service
    predicates:
      - Path=/users/**
```
Client calls:
```text
http://localhost:8080/users/all
```
Gateway rule: path starts with `/users` → go to user-service.

Locator can be OFF (`enabled: false`). Still works.

────────────────────────────────────
Way B — Discovery Locator (AUTO routes)
```yaml
spring.cloud.gateway.discovery.locator.enabled: true
```
Gateway looks at Eureka and auto-creates routes like:
```text
/user-service/**  → lb://user-service
/order-service/** → lb://order-service
```
Client calls:
```text
http://localhost:8080/user-service/all
http://localhost:8080/order-service/list
```
No need to write each `routes:` entry for basic forwarding.

────────────────────────────────────
Easy memory trick:
| Mode                         | Who creates the route? | Example URL                      |
| ---------------------------- | ---------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Explicit `routes:`           | You                    | `/users/**` → user-service       |
| Discovery locator `enabled`  | Gateway (auto)         | `/user-service/**` → user-service |

Important:
- Locator is OPTIONAL
- `lb://` + Eureka works with explicit routes even when locator is false
- Locator is only a shortcut for auto `/service-id/**` URLs

Interview one-liner:
Discovery locator auto-exposes every Eureka service as `/service-name/**`.
Explicit routes let you choose nicer paths like `/users/**`.


🔹 3. Main class
```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class ApiGatewayApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(ApiGatewayApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```
No `@EnableEurekaClient` needed in modern Spring Cloud (starter auto-configures it).


🔹 4. Register downstream services in Eureka
Each service needs:
```yaml
spring:
  application:
    name: user-service   # must match lb://user-service

eureka:
  client:
    service-url:
      defaultZone: http://localhost:8761/eureka/
```
and dependency `spring-cloud-starter-netflix-eureka-client`.


🧪 Test
```text
http://localhost:8080/users/all
http://localhost:8080/orders/list
```
Gateway matches route → discovers instances → load-balances → forwards.


✅ Setup summary
| Step             | What to do                                              |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| Dependencies     | Gateway + Eureka Client (+ LoadBalancer)                |
| Routes           | `uri: lb://service-name` + Path predicate               |
| Discovery        | Services registered in Eureka with correct app name     |
| Locator          | Optional auto-routing; not required if routes are explicit |


🎤 Quick interview Qs
Q: Why `lb://` instead of `http://localhost:8081`?
A: So Gateway discovers instances dynamically and load-balances; no hardcoded host/port.

Q: Gateway vs Eureka?
A: Eureka = service phone-book. Gateway = front door / router that can use that phone-book.

Q: Predicate vs Filter?
A: Predicate = “Does this route match?” Filter = “Change request/response / add behavior.”
